  "operation": {
    "kind": "prompt",
    "user": "Judge these features. Structure is checked by the machine; judge coverage and substance.\n\n## The issue\n\n{{.inputs.issue}}\n\n## The features\n\n{{.inputs.features}}\n\n## The docs\n\n{{.inputs.feature_docs}}\n\n## The paths\n\n{{.inputs.doc_paths}}\n\n## The amendment submitted\n\n{{.inputs.standing_amendment}}\n\n## The docs this phase reads\n\n{{.inputs.working_set}}\n\n## The previous pass\n\n{{.inputs.prior_findings}}\n\nEach carries the draft's disposition. `fixed`: check it. `rejected`: read the note and let it go if it holds. `blocked`: decide whether a person has to answer; if so, `target: ask`.\n\n## What to check\n\n- Each feature is high level: an outcome a customer would name, true under any redesign of the screens. \"Work at a glance\" is a feature; \"you can see the tasks in a menu\" is a pattern that delivers it. A doc that describes a list, an order, a row, a click, a menu, a panel, a surface or a depth describes a UX pattern, and that is a blocker: name the feature the pattern belongs to.\n- The body of each doc is at the feature's altitude, not only its title. A sentence that says what the product does (groups, orders, ranks, remembers, notifies, keeps asking, lets the person choose an arrangement) is behaviour, and behaviour is a pattern: blocker, quote the sentence. A reader who had never seen the issue must not be able to tell from the docs which mechanisms it asked for.\n- The count fits the issue. One issue is usually one feature, sometimes two. Three or more from one issue, or two a marketing page could not tell apart, means the split was by interface: significant, and name the one feature they are.\n- Every goal in the issue is delivered by a feature. Every feature delivers a goal in the issue.\n- Each feature is the smallest marketable unit, measured in outcomes and never in interface. Two outcomes a customer would name as one are one feature; one that a customer would name as two is two; a slice of a screen is neither.\n- No two stories are the same story in different words.\n- Each story's value is something the person wanted before the feature existed.\n- Each metric moves only if the value is delivered, not merely if the feature is used.\n- The `1.0` features alone are worth shipping.\n- The docs follow the writing standard: headings state conclusions, nothing is process, nothing names code, nothing is a question, nothing is argued.\n- Departures from the principles are stated in a line, or absent.\n\n## What to produce\n\n`findings`: each `{ severity: blocker | significant | minor | note, claim, evidence, target_id }`. `evidence` quotes the doc.\n\n`max_severity` and `max_severity_rank` (blocker 3, significant 2, minor 1, note 0). No findings: `note`, 0.\n\n`target`: `draft` when the framing holds and the execution does not; `explore` when the framing is wrong; `upstream` when the issue itself is the problem; `ask` when a person has to decide.\n\nA finding costs a pass. Raise only what changes what gets built.\n",
    "config": {
      "model": "claude-opus-5"
    },
    "input": {},
    "output": {
      "name": "output",
      "kind": "json",
      "schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "findings": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "required": [
                "severity",
                "claim"
              ],
              "properties": {
                "severity": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "enum": [
                    "blocker",
                    "significant",
                    "minor",
                    "note"
                  ]
                },
                "claim": {
